                    "comment": "t.permute - put the axes in a new order"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 963,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 996,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Puts a tensor's axes in a new order, all at once.\n\nYou list every axis by its old number, in the order you want them: for a tensor of shape [2, 7, 6], permute 1 2 0 gives [7, 6, 2] - old axis 1 first, then old axis 2, then old axis 0. The numbers move with their axes; nothing is lost or added.\n\nThe everyday use is changing a layout convention. Image libraries disagree: some keep colour channels first ([channels, height, width]), others last ([height, width, channels]). permute 1 2 0 turns the first into the second, permute 2 0 1 the second into the first. The same goes for [batch, time, joint] versus [batch, joint, time]. For a 2-D tensor, permute 1 0 is simply t.t.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.permute <axis> <axis> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.permute 1 2 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\n\npermute (text box, also an inlet):\nThe new order, as whole numbers in any form (1 2 0, [1, 2, 0], 1,2,0). It must name EVERY axis exactly once. If it lists the wrong number of axes the tensor is passed through UNCHANGED, silently - check the shape when a permute seems to do nothing. A repeated axis prints a 'Permute Error' and sends nothing. Negative numbers count from the end. An empty box passes the tensor through. Changing it does NOT send by itself.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npermuted tensor out:\nThe tensor with its axes reordered. Like t.t, this changes how the memory is read rather than moving it, so the result is usually not contiguous: t.view will refuse it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_layers holds two pictures as one tensor [2, 7, 6]: layer 0 is the F, layer 1 a ramp. The top heat_map shows them one above the other. t.permute 1 2 0 moves the layer axis to the end: [7, 6, 2], a 7 by 6 picture with two values in every cell, like an image with two channels. The two lower heat_maps take channel 0 and channel 1 out again (t[] :, :, 0 and :, :, 1) - the F and the ramp, intact. Click 2 1 0: rows and columns swap as well ([6, 7, 2]) and both pictures come out turned on their side.\n\nRELATED:\nt.transpose swaps just two axes; t.t and t.adjoint swap the last two. t.reshape changes the shape without moving numbers between axes. np.transpose (with an axes list) is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 980,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Puts a tensor's axes in a new order, all at once.\n\nYou list every axis by its old number, in the order you want them: for a tensor of shape [2, 7, 6], permute 1 2 0 gives [7, 6, 2] - old axis 1 first, then old axis 2, then old axis 0. The numbers move with their axes; nothing is lost or added.\n\nThe everyday use is changing a layout convention. Image libraries disagree: some keep colour channels first ([channels, height, width]), others last ([height, width, channels]). permute 1 2 0 turns the first into the second, permute 2 0 1 the second into the first. The same goes for [batch, time, joint] versus [batch, joint, time]. For a 2-D tensor, permute 1 0 is simply t.t.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.permute <axis> <axis> ...\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.permute 1 2 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send.\n\npermute (text box, also an inlet):\nThe new order, as whole numbers in any form (1 2 0, [1, 2, 0], 1,2,0). It must name EVERY axis exactly once. If it lists the wrong number of axes the tensor is passed through UNCHANGED, silently - check the shape when a permute seems to do nothing. A repeated axis prints a 'Permute Error' and sends nothing. Negative numbers count from the end. An empty box passes the tensor through. Changing it does NOT send by itself.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npermuted tensor out:\nThe tensor with its axes reordered. Like t.t, this changes how the memory is read rather than moving it, so the result is usually not contiguous: t.view will refuse it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_layers holds two pictures as one tensor [2, 7, 6]: layer 0 is the F, layer 1 a ramp. The top heat_map shows them one above the other. t.permute 1 2 0 moves the layer axis to the end: [7, 6, 2], a 7 by 6 picture with two values in every cell, like an image with two channels. The two lower heat_maps take channel 0 and channel 1 out again (t[] :, :, 0 and :, :, 1) - the F and the ramp, intact. Click 2 1 0: rows and columns swap as well ([6, 7, 2]) and both pictures come out turned on their side.\n\nRELATED:\nt.transpose swaps just two axes; t.t and t.adjoint swap the last two. t.reshape changes the shape without moving numbers between axes. np.transpose (with an axes list) is the numpy version."
                },
